---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Why Most Service Business Ads Fail — Fornax Blog">
  <article class="post">
    <p class="post-date">September 15, 2026</p>
    <h1>Why Most Service Business Ads Fail (And What to Fix First)</h1>

    <p>
      Every plumber, electrician or HVAC company that's tried paid ads has had the same experience: the clicks come in,
      the budget drains, and the phone doesn't ring nearly as much as the click count suggests it should. The instinct
      is to blame the ad — the headline, the targeting, the platform. Usually that's not where the problem is.
    </p>

    <h2>The click isn't the conversion</h2>
    <p>
      A click gets someone to a page. What happens on that page decides whether they become a lead. If the page loads
      slowly, doesn't clearly state what you do and where you serve, or buries the phone number and contact form below
      three scrolls of stock photography, the ad did its job and the page didn't do its.
    </p>

    <h2>Three places leads actually get lost</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Load time.</strong> A few extra seconds of load time on mobile is enough for a impatient searcher to hit back and call the next result instead.</li>
      <li><strong>Unclear service area.</strong> If it's not obvious in the first screen that you serve their neighborhood, they assume you don't.</li>
      <li><strong>Too many steps to contact you.</strong> Every extra field on a form, or every extra click to find a phone number, loses a percentage of visitors.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Fix the landing experience before you scale the spend</h2>
    <p>
      Spending more on ads before fixing a slow, unclear landing page just buys more of the same low conversion rate at
      a higher cost. A fast, accessible, trade-specific site with a phone number and short form above the fold usually
      moves the needle more than a bigger budget on the same underperforming page.
    </p>

    <p>
      This is the core of what a generated, trade-specific site is built to solve — speed and clarity checked before
      the site ever reaches a customer, not bolted on after the fact.
    </p>
  </article>

  <section class="cta-footer">
    <h2>See what a fast, trade-specific site looks like</h2>
    <a href="/demo" class="button button-primary">Get my demo</a>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .post {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 60px 20px;
  }

  .post-date {
    font-size: 13px;
    color: #999;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.3px;
    margin: 0 0 12px;
  }

  .post h1 {
    font-size: 34px;
    line-height: 1.15;
    margin: 0 0 32px;
  }

  .post h2 {
    font-size: 22px;
    margin: 36px 0 16px;
  }

  .post p {
    color: #444;
    line-height: 1.75;
    margin: 0 0 18px;
  }

  .post ul {
    color: #444;
    line-height: 1.8;
    padding-left: 20px;
    margin: 0 0 18px;
  }

  .cta-footer {
    text-align: center;
    background: #f9f9f9;
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 48px 20px;
    max-width: 1200px;
    margin: 0 auto 60px;
  }

  .cta-footer h2 {
    font-size: 26px;
    margin: 0 0 20px;
  }

  .button {
    padding: 14px 32px;
    border-radius: 6px;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-decoration: none;
    border: none;
    cursor: pointer;
    font-size: 16px;
    display: inline-block;
  }

  .button-primary {
    background: #dc2626;
    color: white;
  }

  .button-primary:hover {
    background: #b91c1c;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
</style>
